spec ww05-update-delete-chain-rollback: 2 sessions, 10 permutations
# WW05, rollback arm. s1 updates n then deletes it in ONE transaction and ROLLS
# BACK; the vacuum is drained immediately after (H1). s2, whose snapshot predates
# s1's chain, writes n.y. s2 is refused while the chain is in flight and allowed
# after the rollback and drain; n survives with x unchanged. PostgreSQL: the waiter
# proceeds once the DELETE aborts — the same verdict, without the wait.
# The second writer is an EXPLICIT transaction that BEGINs before
# either session's first step, so its snapshot predates the ROLLBACK. A ROLLBACK
# aborts its transaction (rmp #2973): once it is drained, the older snapshot's write
# must succeed, exactly as the autocommit arm's does. Before #2973 the ROLLBACK
# published a commit record and this write was refused.

starting permutation: s1u s1d s1rb s1dr s2w s2c
step s1u: MATCH (n:Item {name:'n'}) SET n.x = 1 RETURN n.x AS x
x
-
1
(1 row)
step s1d: MATCH (n:Item {name:'n'}) DETACH DELETE n RETURN count(*) AS deleted
deleted
-------
1      
(1 row)
step s1rb: ROLLBACK
step s1dr: <vacuum drain>
step s2w: MATCH (n:Item {name:'n'}) SET n.y = 1 RETURN n.y AS y
y
-
1
(1 row)
step s2c: COMMIT
final final: MATCH (n:Item) RETURN n.name AS name, n.x AS x, n.y AS y ORDER BY name
name|x|y
----+-+-
"n" |0|1
(1 row)

